#!/bin/sh
# Adjust the touch/pen orientation at runtime (calibration helper).
# The panel is portrait (1200x1920) but the Novatek touch reports in landscape
# (1920x1200), so a 90-degree transform is needed. Find the direction that
# makes taps land where you touch.
#
#   ct-next-touch ccw    # 90 deg counter-clockwise (current default)
#   ct-next-touch cw     # 90 deg clockwise
#   ct-next-touch 180    # upside down
#   ct-next-touch none   # no rotation
#   ct-next-touch horiz  # mirrored horizontally (of none)
#   ct-next-touch vert   # mirrored vertically (of none)
#
# To make it permanent, put the printed matrix into
# /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/20-touch.conf (Option "TransformationMatrix").
export DISPLAY=:0
case "$1" in
	none) M="1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1" ;;
	cw)   M="0 -1 1 1 0 0 0 0 1" ;;
	ccw)  M="0 1 0 -1 0 1 0 0 1" ;;
	180)  M="-1 0 1 0 -1 1 0 0 1" ;;
	horiz) M="-1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 1" ;;
	vert) M="1 0 0 0 -1 1 0 0 1" ;;
	*) echo "usage: ct-next-touch none|cw|ccw|180|horiz|vert" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
for name in NVTCapacitiveTouchScreen NVTCapacitivePen; do
	if xinput set-prop "$name" "Coordinate Transformation Matrix" $M 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "$name: $1 ($M)"
	else
		echo "$name: not found" >&2
	fi
done
